7] [17592186115299 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115299 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115299 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115299 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115299 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1045 "Gaussian Elimination For NxM Systems of Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115479 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3b658871-23f8-5b8d-92ee-5294fec9bba3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115830 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115830 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115830 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n${x + 3z =-2; x + y = 0$.\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115830 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8cd9c6c5-780e-5f01-93cc-d5e9b9533129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116120 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116120 #edb/ref 1061 17213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116120 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6022e9b9-60ab-5ac5-b16a-17583fa649c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116120 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116121 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116121 #edb/ref 1061 17214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116121 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79bb6bd1-8c36-5f56-9575-8a1b8388eb57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116121 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116122 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116122 #edb/ref 1061 17215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116122 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2fa6eba1-6298-51b5-bbc9-bd8c46a79468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116122 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116123 #edb/ref 1061 17216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116123 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d1a97f8-c872-5285-b963-d1938948df37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116123 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116209 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116209 #edb/ref 1061 23072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116209 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e80cfcd9-1219-52d8-96c7-0ee06cdf52fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116209 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116493 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use Gaussian elimination to find the general solutions of systems with any number of equations and variables. For instance, consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z=1, \\\\ y+3z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has the augmented matrix\n![](images/8a/8a8452587098eed9b697c3999c356359b949429f29ce16af2c81cf728be9480e.svg)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\, & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+4z=1 \\\\ y+3z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable $z.$ From the second equation, we get $y=-3z,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x=1-4z.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-4z, \\\\ y=-3z, \\\\ z=z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-4z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116493 #edb/ref 1065 3162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116493 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3eff050b-e4ba-52e5-aa10-7bba2feef2fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116493 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116494 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a system of linear equations is called **consistent** if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, if there are no solutions, the system is called **inconsistent**. \n\nFor a consistent system, \n\n- the system is called **consistent independent** if the solution is unique, while\n\n- the system is called **consistent dependent** if there are infinitely many solutions.\n\n![](images/f5/f5b808eac6e1d865b13b68ce2a4f7d317560ae3965928e478531fcd2148c2c6b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116494 #edb/ref 1065 3163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116494 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e0c85152-daea-5a87-a3ba-81870558c7a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116494 #edb/ref 1067 "Consistent and Inconsistent Systems of Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343154 #edb/ref 1080 "k=5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343154 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343154 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6b9a3770-af37-58af-91e7-3430fd2463b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343221 #edb/ref 1080 "k=3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343221 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343221 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "71cae964-c567-5585-ab23-7241fb114732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343724 #edb/ref 1080 "k=1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343724 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343724 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a8f3da90-4b17-5b45-af78-2746f79a0b95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343764 #edb/ref 1080 "k=9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343764 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343764 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ad97781e-f202-56a0-803a-f17ee1a49ca9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344356 #edb/ref 1080 "k=7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344356 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344356 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f0d5b11f-4138-5c57-8bc8-f9f3a404cfaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344664 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344664 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344664 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344664 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344664 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344664 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344664 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344664 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186344356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344664 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6517af05-6089-578f-972a-9310f8010c94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344961 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344961 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74646" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344961 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344961 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=2 \\\\  & 2x+(k+1)y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344961 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0c28bd7-70de-59aa-93c9-933cbaca4593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344961 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-7 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+4y=2 \\\\ (k-7)y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=7,$ then the second equation is $0=-1,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400646 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186438977 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186400646 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186438978 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186400646 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186438979 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186400646 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400646 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400646 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 3x-3y=6 \\\\  & -2x+y=-3 \\\\  & 4x-4y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The system is consistent dependent\n- **II.** The system is consistent independent\n- **III.** The system has no solution" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186400646 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4890e1ff-ed9a-5558-acd1-bcef01439e04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400646 #edb/ref 1074 "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=2 \\\\ y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we have $y=-1$ and substituting this into the first equation yields $x=1.$ So,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186425937 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425937 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425937 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425937 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425937 #edb/ref 1045 "Gaussian Elimination For NxM Systems of Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425937 #edb/ref 1